 "notes": "Both arms saw identical placements and identical direction streams and differ only in the return ARS optimised; both terminal means were frozen in finalists.json before any confirmation case. Deviation: during confirmation one evaluation process (the reference on manifest s4825, the tenth cell) died at simulator start-up with 'malloc(): unaligned tcache chunk detected' before producing any result; the driver was rerun, skipped the 9 finished cells and ran the remaining 39; no training child was relaunched and nothing was re-scored (log kept as driver_crash_s4825.log). The dense arm optimises R-103's summed step reward and still raises the success count as much as the exact arm does: in training the two arms' candidate success curves are indistinguishable (0.39 -> 0.51) and each arm's finalist also scores well on the other arm's return; the two finalists point the same way (cosine 0.84). So D-075's gain is not explained by the exact-success objective; what R-106 and both arms here share, and R-103 lacked, is the instrument: 512 shared placements per signed candidate in one batch (A/A slot differences at most 0.016 here) and 17.6 M transitions per run, 38x R-103's. This also revises D-074's side remark: with a quiet enough contrast, the dense reward's policy-level misalignment does not stop ARS from finding success gains. The frozen R-106 finalist replicates on entirely new cases: +0.110 [+0.080, +0.140] (828 vs 659), descriptive, outside the Holm family. One matched pair of trajectories; charged starts 217,323 of 250,000; zero LLM calls."
